  "metadata": {
    "title": "Dear Apple Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for June 8",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "This article first appeared on GuruFocus.\nApple (NASDAQ:AAPL) heads into its Worldwide Developers Conference this week with investors looking for fresh details on the company's artificial intelligence strategy, following a strong fiscal second quarter and growing expectations around Apple Intelligence.\nApple has positioned WWDC as a showcase for software updates, AI features and developer tools. The event comes as the company works to expand its AI offerings after facing questions about rollout timelines and execution. Apple recently resolved litigation tied to delays in Siri-related AI features, highlighting the importance of upcoming product announcements.\nApple reported fiscal second-quarter revenue of $111.2 billion, up 17% from a year earlier. Net income rose to $29.6 billion from $24.8 billion, while diluted earnings per share increased 22% to $2.01. Apple said products generated $80.2 billion in revenue, while services revenue reached a record $31 billion.\nApple also produced more than $28 billion in operating cash flow during the quarter and ended the period with $45.6 billion in cash and equivalents. For the first six months of fiscal 2026, free cash flow totaled about $78.3 billion.\nWall Street remains broadly positive on Apple. Morgan Stanley, Goldman Sachs, Bank of America and Wedbush have all maintained bullish views, with analysts pointing to AI initiatives as a potential long-term growth driver for Apple.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "This article first appeared on GuruFocus.",
      "Apple (NASDAQ:AAPL) heads into its Worldwide Developers Conference this week with investors looking for fresh details on the company's artificial intelligence strategy, following a strong fiscal second quarter and growing expectations around Apple Intelligence.",
      "Apple has positioned WWDC as a showcase for software updates, AI features and developer tools. The event comes as the company works to expand its AI offerings after facing questions about rollout timelines and execution. Apple recently resolved litigation tied to delays in Siri-related AI features, highlighting the importance of upcoming product announcements.",
      "Apple reported fiscal second-quarter revenue of $111.2 billion, up 17% from a year earlier. Net income rose to $29.6 billion from $24.8 billion, while diluted earnings per share increased 22% to $2.01. Apple said products generated $80.2 billion in revenue, while services revenue reached a record $31 billion.",
      "Apple also produced more than $28 billion in operating cash flow during the quarter and ended the period with $45.6 billion in cash and equivalents. For the first six months of fiscal 2026, free cash flow totaled about $78.3 billion.",
      "Wall Street remains broadly positive on Apple. Morgan Stanley, Goldman Sachs, Bank of America and Wedbush have all maintained bullish views, with analysts pointing to AI initiatives as a potential long-term growth driver for Apple."
    ],
